    {
      "raw_rank": 6,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00103",
      "distance": 0.7931903600692749,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 177,
      "page_end": 177,
      "text": "There is thus another dimension to persuasion that is as fundamental as the issue Davis hosts for us. Sociality resides. It is always somewhere, and this somewhere can never be fully circumscribed by either meaning or symbolicity. We are worldly. Necessarily, our a priori affectability is not just a transaction among animate beings-humans primarily, but as Davis suggests, also nonhuman animals-so that we see this affectability as enmeshed in material locales that give place and bearing prior to symbolicity proper. All being must be part of the giving of what gives, and the world is what is given, both as meaning and as what withdraws from meaning, even ifworld is not equivalently given. Thus, the challenge of this chapter is to think how rhetoric is caught up in the play of language and materiality while raising the specter that neither language nor materiality can be reduced to symbolicity. Indeed, that is the problem: to what extent can we discuss materiality prior to meaning or symbolicity, and how can we discuss what gives rise to symbolicity as part of symbolicity itself? These questions are themselves problematic from Burke's perspective. Burke almost uniformly places meaning first, and the rhetoricity implicit to symbolic action emerges with it. Heidegger, too, can be and frequently has been read as offering this position, which thus has been taken to mean that world, while certainly material, is always already and only one of significance, making\n\nworld inseparable from meaning. I, however, am going to argue that while the world is a world of involvements, and hence meaning, world is also involved in those involvements in ways that are never captured, submerged, or rendered secondary to world as it emerges for human being. This sense of world requires a rich materiality that bears up and stays within human sociality and significance, so that it must also be understood as constituting affective relationality, without, for all that, collapsing into human sociality and significance. Such materiality, as I will show in the upcoming chapters, should always be understood across the play of presence and withdrawal, and human being is only one locus for this play.\n\nWhen I claim rhetoric is ontological, then, I mean that (1) we come to see that rhetoric cannot be sundered from material being or reduced to epistemological considerations; (2) we understand that rhetoric is intimate with the environments in which it emerges (and not just to which it is joined, as in two separate realms coming together), which grants non humans an elemental role in rhetoricity; (3) this intimacy is not solely a matter of human projection, control. or assignment; and (4) grappling with these entangled, mutually coevolving, and transformative interactions among persons, world, and discourses requires a new appreciation for their constitutive complexity. I can reduce this to a somewhat more succinct statement: rhetoric is a responsive way of revealing the world for others, responding to and put forth through affective, symbolic, and material means, so as to (at least potentially) reattune or otherwise transform how others inhabit the world to an extent that calls for some action (which can include, of course, steadfastness, refusal, or even apathy). Beyond resonating with Davis's approach, my line of thought also bears an affinity to George Kennedy's important essay \"A Hoot in the Dark,\" where Kennedy claims that rhetoric is energy (2). I augment Kennedy, however, by adding that energy is always materialized. Energy emerges within and transforms how one dwells in the world or at least allows for that world to show up, to reveal itself, differently. Ifwe are going to keep to persuasion, then, this is a further intensification of it. Persuasion thought ambiently looks to a materialist affectability that sustains our being-in-the-world. An ambient rhetoric will have taken things at their world and notjust their word.\n\nPerhaps counterintuitively, I want to begin by opening the question not with materiality itself but with language's relation to world, thought most consequentially as how the world manifests itself to us as understandable. I will address also the withdrawal of meaning, although such a withdrawal",
